{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Social metadata
  Phase 16. Open Graph and Twitter card tags, emitted once from the head.

  Accepts:
  - nothing. Everything is read from the page's own Shopify objects.

  Usage:
  {% render 'meta-social' %}

  WHY THIS EXISTS.
  Phase 16 measured the rendered head of all seven templates and found zero
  Open Graph tags, zero Twitter tags and no apple-touch-icon. Without them a
  link to this store pasted into Messenger, Viber, Instagram, X or Slack shows
  whatever the platform can scrape — usually the raw URL and a random image, or
  nothing. For a brand whose customers share on social, that is the first
  impression of the store.

  EVERY VALUE COMES FROM SHOPIFY. NOTHING HERE IS WRITTEN COPY.
  The title is page_title, the description is page_description, the URL is
  canonical_url and the site name is shop.name — the same objects the <title>
  and the canonical already use, so the card can never disagree with the page.
  There is no hand-written marketing sentence in this file and no invented
  claim about the brand.

  THE IMAGE CHAIN, MOST SPECIFIC FIRST.
    product page     the product's own featured image
    collection page  the collection's image, when the merchant has set one
    anything else    the merchant's share image, then the logo
  Each step is guarded, so a store with no images at any level emits no og:image
  at all rather than a broken URL. An og:image that 404s is worse than none: the
  platform caches the failure.

  1200px WIDE, NOT THE ORIGINAL.
  Facebook and X want roughly 1200x630. Serving the original would push a
  multi-megabyte photograph to every scraper. The width/height tags carry the
  real dimensions of the version being served, which is what stops the platforms
  re-fetching to measure it.

  NO twitter:image.
  X falls back to og:image for a summary_large_image card, so a second copy of
  the same URL is a tag that can only ever drift out of step with the first.
  Dawn makes the same choice.

  NO og:price.
  The product's price and availability are already published, correctly and in
  the format search engines actually consume, by the structured_data filter in
  sections/main-product.liquid. A second copy in a different vocabulary is a
  second thing to keep in step, and the OG product-price tags are legacy.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign og_type = 'website'
  if request.page_type == 'product'
    assign og_type = 'product'
  elsif request.page_type == 'article'
    assign og_type = 'article'
  endif

  assign share_image = blank
  if request.page_type == 'product' and product.featured_media != blank
    assign share_image = product.featured_media.preview_image
  elsif request.page_type == 'collection' and collection.featured_image != blank
    assign share_image = collection.featured_image
  elsif settings.share_image != blank
    assign share_image = settings.share_image
  elsif settings.logo != blank
    assign share_image = settings.logo
  endif
-%}

<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ shop.name | escape }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical_url }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ page_title | escape }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="{{ og_type }}">
{%- if page_description != blank %}
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
{%- endif %}

{%- if share_image != blank -%}
  <meta property="og:image" content="http:{{ share_image | image_url: width: 1200 }}">
  <meta property="og:image:secure_url" content="https:{{ share_image | image_url: width: 1200 }}">

  {%- comment -%}
    The dimensions of the file actually being served, derived from the source
    image's own width and height.

    NOT from aspect_ratio. The first version of this divided 1200 by
    share_image.aspect_ratio, and the Phase 16 head audit failed on every one of
    the seven templates with a division by zero: aspect_ratio is not populated on
    every image drop, and Liquid treats the missing value as 0. Shopify would
    have thrown the same error on a live store — on every page, in the head.

    width is always present on a real image and is never zero, so dividing by it
    is safe. It is still guarded, because an empty drop reaches here as 0 too and
    the point of these tags is to stop a scraper re-fetching the image — not
    worth taking the page down for.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if share_image.width > 0 and share_image.height > 0 -%}
    {%- assign share_height = share_image.height | times: 1200 | divided_by: share_image.width -%}
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="{{ share_height }}">
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if share_image.alt != blank %}
    <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ share_image.alt | escape }}">
  {%- endif %}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  summary_large_image only when there is an image to be large. With no image the
  card type promises a picture the scraper will not find, and X renders the
  degraded version — so the plain summary card is the honest choice there.
{%- endcomment -%}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="{% if share_image != blank %}summary_large_image{% else %}summary{% endif %}">
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ page_title | escape }}">
{%- if page_description != blank %}
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
{%- endif %}
